1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Every goal starts the same studio argument: why was he free?
The replay shows what happened. It doesn't show why.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Chance Genesis watches 25 hertz tracking data. When a shot happens, it traces the previous ten seconds,
finds the cause, and has a verified explanation ready before the replay starts.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Here, the striker's run drags his marker fifteen metres wide.
The yellow area is the space that opened.

4
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The dashed circle is where the defender would have been: the receiver would have had 33 square metres instead of 127.
And the verdict: he followed his man. Pulled out by design, not out of position.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:53,000
For fans at home, the same checked facts become a story about the key player.

6
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:06,000
This chance looks the same on replay, but the tracking disagrees:
the marker stopped for two seconds while his man sprinted. That's a lost marker, not a decoy.

7
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Here the press closed the defender down from fifteen metres to two,
cut his passing lanes, and won the ball 35 metres from goal.

8
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The language model only writes the words. Every number, time and player comes from code,
and a verifier rejects any draft that adds a fact.

9
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:36,000
On 280 held-out test scenarios, including traps designed to fool it,
it names the right cause 94 percent of the time, with one percent false alarms.

10
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:52,000
It's built with Microsoft Agent Framework: a narrator, a fan commentator and a tactical analyst,
checked by a verifier with retry and fallback, running on Microsoft Foundry and Azure App Service.

11
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Chance Genesis. The why, before the replay ends.
